content = "# Tastylive (Tom Sosnoff) Most Used Option Strategies\n\nSource: shared by Mike from a tastylive video summary, 2026-08-02. These are described as the boring but effective strategies used by professional retail traders per this source.\n\nSaved for future use: when Mikes scanning system identifies candidate trades, cross check the candidate against these parameters before presenting it as genuinely aligned with tastylive methodology.\n\n## Strategies To Get Long\n\n### 1. Short Puts\nSimplest and most capital efficient strategy. Sell OTM puts 35 to 50 DTE. Target 16 to 22 deltas at the expected move, buying power reduction of 20 percent of strike price. Best for beaten down stocks with IVR 30 percent or more. Profit target 50 percent of premium collected.\n\n### 2. Jade Lizard\nShort put combined with short call spread kicker. Position so there is no upside risk, achieved by ensuring total premium collected exceeds the width of the strikes on the short call spread. Approximately 40 DTE, sell OTM put and call spread just inside expected move. Profit target approximately 50 percent of total premium.\n\n### 3. Covered Calls\nClassic basis reduction trade. Execute as a single trade, stock and options together, on stocks with low price and high implied volatility. 40 to 60 DTE, short strike between 25 and 30 delta. Consider taking profits if short strike is breached, always close as a single trade.\n\n### 4. Short Put Spreads\nDefined risk alternative for expensive stocks. Target credit between 30 and 35 percent of width of strikes. Profit target 50 percent of credit received.\n\n### 5. Put Ratio Spreads\nHigh probability strategy for oversold stocks with high IV. Use a 1x2 approach: long strike at expected move, sell twice as many further OTM puts to generate a net credit. Profit target at least the net credit received.\n"
with open('TASTYLIVE_STRATEGIES.md', 'w') as f:
    f.write(content)
print("Chunk 1 written")
